ble from them. They do not attack without a behavioral trigger, but obvious awareness, interference, or attempts to confront them break this tolerance.\n- Each cleaning group carries access keys for the locked service areas, typically in a uniform pocket. Their scheduled appearance makes them both a timer for the feeding cycle and the primary source of the sixth-floor exit key.\n\n##### Patient-Room Alcoves\n- Small side spaces connected directly to the sixth-floor feeding corridor and populated by the same tranquilized patients. They provide concealment but no alternate exit.\n- An entrant hiding here must continue matching the surrounding patient behavior. Deliberate whispering, checking the corridor, preparing a weapon, or reacting normally to screams can distinguish the entrant from the ward population when staff pass.\n\n##### Director's Engagement Floor\n- Open corridor-and-atrium area used as the Director's principal confrontation space, identifiable by shattered windows, broken ceiling panels, scorched walls, pulverized plaster, and a heavy acrid smell layered over the usual disinfectant.\n- Wide sightlines and limited cover strongly favor the Director.\n\n**Gangbuk Hospital Director (강북 병원장)**\n- A seventy-four-year-old elderly man in a physician's white coat. His upper face appears almost completely ordinary, but the lower jaw hangs unnaturally to the level of his sternum, allowing the mouth to open into an enormous cavity while he continues speaking and observing with clinical composure.\n- Opening the mouth is the clearest attack cue. Jaw closure erases anything fully enclosed from the local physical state rather than biting normally: flesh, limbs inside untouched clothing, wall sections, open air, and incoming projectiles or force can vanish.\n- He moves with extreme short-range speed and may cross an entire room between moments of attention. Objects jammed into the mouth during opening or heavy blows against the hanging lower jaw can interrupt closure, but ordinary attacks do not solve the underlying problem.\n- Harmful objects or effects forced past the jaws remain in his stomachless mouth or throat until expelled and can damage him internally.\n- He treats the sixth-floor population as research stock and evaluates successful experimental results with genuine professional satisfaction. His own altered mouth and taste are regarded not as deformities but as superior sensory organs capable of reading the fundamental nature of whatever he consumes.",
     "entities": "##### False-Sky Resident\n###### Jellyfish (해파리)\n- Luminous entities drifting beneath the false night sky above the sixth floor and deeper hospital spaces. Their presence is consistent with the soul-jellyfish manifestations found throughout other Hospital facilities.\n- They provide a dim blue ambient light and remain visually detached from the immediate feeding cycle. Their exact relationship to Gangbuk's taste research is not externally obvious, but prolonged direct observation should still be treated as hazardous."
