@@ -192,7 +192,7 @@
     "identity": "- A concept-type anomaly distributed through the massively popular musical \"Red Lady.\" Performance, theater, hypnosis, Dream Scenes, and title character together form it; no independent fixed body exists outside production.\n- No fixed script: scenes improvise around viewers, memories, reactions, and the dominant genre. This flexibility is its principal structural weakness.\n- The Theater Troupe propagates it as commercial entertainment and a mass-casualty weapon. Popularity recruits ordinary ticket-seekers into real theaters; entrapment begins only with the performance.",
     "entry": "- Ordinary tickets through purchase, invitation, resale, or legitimate-looking channels admit viewers to a celebrated mainstream show. No anomalous knowledge or ritual is required.\n- Entry occurs inside a real urban theater. Once the curtain rises, exits lock, mobile communication drops completely out of service, and the seated audience becomes subject to the production's collective hypnotic layer.\n- Prior exposure can leave a persistent mark. A person who has previously encountered the Red Lady may retain only the fact that they looked at her and experienced severe pain, while the visual memory of the encounter itself has been removed.",
     "space": "##### Stage Reality\n- The auditorium and stage initially resemble a polished professional musical production, with elaborate sets, lighting, actors, announcements, and audience management. The underlying horror remains concealed behind normal theatrical procedure until the performance cycle begins.\n- Curtain-up synchronizes hypnosis. Bodies stay seated; consciousness enters individualized Dream Scenes with coherent false identities, histories, relationships, and memories. Viewers experience themselves as characters.\n- Dream Scenes are numbered sequentially and can completely redress between transitions: village streets, forests, cliffs, houses, jungles, childhood locations, or spaces constructed from personal memory can replace one another without physical continuity. Entire subjective lifetimes can occur while little real-world time passes.\n- The production suppresses anomalous abilities that would contradict the active scene's narrative. The Dream Scene also expresses reality in theatrical form: dialogue, action, sound, and environmental events can behave as though transcribed into stage directions, with the scene reorganizing itself around the people currently driving the performance.\n- Interaction is required for scene progression. Dialogue combined with meaningful action advances the production; simply walking in silence does not. A lone viewer placed in a scene without another viable participant can become trapped in one sequence while the Red Lady's entrance window continues to shorten.\n\n##### Auditorium\n- A large tiered theater packed with civilians. When the performance begins, the audience becomes unnaturally quiet and remains seated through events that would normally cause immediate panic, including injuries and deaths nearby.\n- Physical consciousness returns during intermissions. The resulting breaks are chaotic, with survivors screaming, bargaining, crying, or attempting to understand what has happened before the collection announcement resolves exhausted Chances.\n- Purged audience members detonate in place, coating adjacent rows in blood and remains. Their seats and surrounding contamination remain physically present when the next act begins.\n- An awakened viewer's assigned seat remains recognized by the production. Leaving the seating area during restricted portions of the show causes Troupe Security to intercept and redirect the person.\n\n##### Main Stage\n- The visible performance stage continuously redresses according to the current genre and scene: village streets, cottages, forests, domestic interiors, or other settings can replace one another between cues.\n- Actors often stand unnaturally motionless between required lines and transition immediately into fluid performance when their cue arrives, as though movement exists only while the scene requires it.\n- During late-stage genre corruption, ordinary theatrical props can become physically useful weapons within the degraded production logic. Iron bars, bricks, stage furniture, and similar objects may remain mundane but become capable of injuring the Red Lady once her role protection has collapsed.\n\n##### Dream-Scene Layer\n- Hypnotic staging around individuals or small groups fabricates childhoods, loved ones, homes, communities, histories, and tailored conflicts, sustaining character identity over awareness of being viewers.\n- Companions seated side by side may be inserted into completely separate stagings or placed where the musical deliberately forces interaction between them. The production prefers configurations that create dialogue, betrayal, rescue, conflict, or other usable dramatic material.\n- Scene transitions can briefly release consciousness with severe disorientation before another scene starts.\n- A floating exit door can occasionally appear when the current narrative permits departure. Passage is limited and may accept only one person at a time, forcing multiple awakened participants to queue or decide who uses the opening first.\n- Scene environments respond to successful genre hijacking in real time.\n\n##### Backstage and Corridors\n- Narrow red-lit service corridors connect auditorium, stage, dressing areas, and deeper production spaces. They provide a physical route around seating restrictions and can be used once the group has regained enough control to leave the auditorium.\n- Troupe Security patrols these areas and redirects unauthorized audience members. Kidnapped civilians, replacement performers, and other production material can also be moved through the backstage system.",
-    "rules": "##### Chances, Acts, and Genre Corruption\n- Every viewer has unseen Chances. Act 1 dream death returns them physically intact to their auditorium seat at a cost of one; no counter reveals the balance. Preserve Chances and use death only as emergency ejection.\n- At intermission, hypnosis lifts simultaneously and spent viewers are collected. An announcement declares that those who have exhausted their Chances will \"join the Red Lady,\" after which affected audience members explode in their seats. Early intermissions may remove roughly one fifth of the audience, while later collection cycles can eliminate most remaining viewers.\n- Ejection from an Act 1 scene preserves ordinary scene memories but removes any direct visual memory of the Red Lady. The resulting gap remains associated with pain, fear, or physical sensation without recoverable visual content.\n- Deliberate scene death can eject an Act 1 viewer, whether by fatal fall, self-inflicted trauma, or another character. It still costs a Chance and becomes riskier as the hidden balance falls.\n- Act 2 removes this safety mechanism. Suicide or intentional death during Act 2 is actual death and does not return the subject to the auditorium; surviving Act 2 requires reaching a legitimate scene exit or completing the performance's altered route.\n- Staff truthfully confirm there is no fixed script. Use Act 1 to gather information, then repeatedly impose comedy, romance, action spectacle, philosophical debate, game logic, or other incompatible genres through coordinated dialogue, behavior, and scene direction. The production must accommodate sufficiently strong improvisation, redirecting sets, supporting characters, pacing, and antagonist. Accumulated disruption makes it too incoherent or aesthetically poor to sustain an unbeatable horror lead; maintain corruption until the Red Lady becomes physically vulnerable.",
+    "rules": "##### Chances, Acts, and Genre Corruption\n- Every viewer has unseen Chances. Act 1 dream death returns them physically intact to their auditorium seat at a cost of one; no counter reveals the balance. Preserve Chances and use death only as emergency ejection.\n- At intermission, hypnosis lifts simultaneously and spent viewers are collected. An announcement declares that those who have exhausted their Chances will \"join the Red Lady,\" after which affected audience members explode in their seats. Early intermissions may remove roughly one fifth of the audience, while later collection cycles can eliminate most remaining viewers.\n- Ejection from an Act 1 scene preserves ordinary scene memories but removes any direct visual memory of the Red Lady.\n- Act 2 removes this safety mechanism. Suicide or intentional death during Act 2 is actual death and does not return the subject to the auditorium; surviving Act 2 requires reaching a legitimate scene exit or completing the performance's altered route.\n- Staff truthfully confirm there is no fixed script. Use Act 1 to gather information, then repeatedly impose comedy, romance, action spectacle, philosophical debate, game logic, or other incompatible genres through coordinated dialogue, behavior, and scene direction. The production must accommodate sufficiently strong improvisation, redirecting sets, supporting characters, pacing, and antagonist. Accumulated disruption makes it too incoherent or aesthetically poor to sustain an unbeatable horror lead; maintain corruption until the Red Lady becomes physically vulnerable.",
     "entities": "##### Red Lady\n- The central role of the production and the primary embodiment of its horror logic. She appears as a woman slightly taller than an average adult man, wearing a red dress and approaching with slow, deliberate footsteps from darkness rather than rushing or chasing.\n- Her face above the mouth cannot be perceptually resolved. It is not merely blurred or physically damaged; observation returns no stable information, as though the relevant region has been removed from visual reality. Despite this, observers somehow know with certainty that she is smiling.\n- Direct observation causes immediate eye damage, including intense burning pain and bleeding, and observation-enhancing abilities suffer the same backlash. Even successful witnesses cannot retain her appearance afterward; the portion of memory containing her visual form is cleanly removed.\n- Her entrances follow theatrical timing. Early Dream Scenes allow long periods before she appears, while later scenes shorten the interval. Escape doors and environmental exits can disappear as she approaches, allowing the stage itself to preserve her entrance and prevent an easy scene break.\n- While a coherent horror musical defines her as its \"overwhelming slaughterer,\" narrative authority, reality interference, and perceptual control defeat conventional attacks, anomalous abilities, and direct overpowering. Sufficient genre corruption makes her a physical ordinary villain on stage or in the auditorium: face safely visible, reality interference sharply weakened. A transient red throat mark is the only effective physical weak point; coordinated attacks there with available weapons can end the performance.\n\n##### Troupe Actors\n- Ordinary humans originally recruited as aspiring performers and subsequently enslaved into the production. Their bodies may remain functional despite injuries incompatible with normal stage performance, including severely displaced necks or other deformations.\n- They speak and move according to cue, sometimes stuttering or fragmenting between lines, and can remain motionless until required by the scene. Despite their anomalous continued performance, their bodies retain normal human physical vulnerability and can be killed through ordinary lethal force.\n\n##### Troupe Security\n- Former venue workers or temporary staff incorporated into the production as enforcement personnel. They may continue walking, speaking, and working with necks bent or rotated into anatomically lethal positions.\n- Their function is audience control rather than direct theatrical performance: they intercept viewers leaving assigned seats, redirect wanderers, guard service corridors, and prevent premature disruption of the show.\n- They remain physically human despite their condition. Lethal force kills them normally."
    },
