     "entry": "- Sleep Abduction: the standard Act Two route. A person selected as cast falls asleep normally and awakens inside the stage already dressed, assigned a role, and embedded in the production's current scene.\n- Street Abduction: Theater Troupe personnel can physically seize a target and throw them through a painted backdrop temporarily manifested against an ordinary wall. The target loses consciousness during transfer and awakens inside the same dream-stage under prepared costuming and role assignment.\n- Entry can include multiple people simultaneously, but the Troupe decides their casting rather than preserving real-world relationships. Participants may awaken as relatives, servants, lovers, rivals, or strangers regardless of who they actually are.",
     "rules": "##### Casting and Scene Continuity\n- The stage presents as a late-medieval or early-modern European aristocratic drama beneath a permanent summer sky: manor interiors, gardens, carriages, cathedral sets, moonlit roads, and later a flooded final stage. Architecture is theatrical rather than complete; details outside the active scene may simply not exist, such as a carriage curtain that cannot be moved because nothing has been rendered beyond it.\n- Cast intuitively perceive one another's assigned roles; compare perceptions to establish the cast before plot advancement.\n- Roles assign clothing, hair, posture, and social position automatically. Speech can be forcibly corrected to the required register even during deliberate defiance.\n- The predetermined script is inaccessible. Apparent rebellion may itself be scripted; early cooperation with blocking and roles can buy time to identify the production mechanism.\n- Scene transitions depend on theatrical attention and interaction rather than physical travel. Dialogue and meaningful action advance scenes, while silent walking alone does not. A set being directly watched remains materially present; when attention leaves it, the production is free to remove or replace it. NPC actors may stand completely motionless between cues and resume fluid behavior the instant a line or stage trigger occurs.\n- Off-view time is discontinuous: cast can reappear without travel, and the production skips periods lacking theatrical necessity.\n- Ordinary communication is suppressed. Phones display no service, while messages or contact from outside may appear only when their arrival would serve the active drama.\n\n##### Confession and Death\n- The central romantic-tragedy rule targets anyone who confesses romantic love to the protagonist's Mistress role. Completing such a confession triggers a severe psychic confirmation attack followed by an escalating compulsory self-destruction impulse; the confessor typically cuts their own throat and dies within seconds regardless of prior intent.\n- The confessor, not the recipient, suffers the compulsion. The Mistress can receive repeated confessions while romantic leads become casualties.\n- Death is not an escape mechanism. A cast member who dies can be converted into a Puppet and returned to the performance under external control, while additional Puppets are generated as scenes continue. Allowing the production to advance on its own script therefore increases the number of hostile bodies rather than reducing them.",
     "space": "##### The Mansion / Mistress's Bedroom\n- The central narrative hub is an aristocratic household dressed with heavy curtains, ornate furniture, candlelight, and an oversized canopy bed. Early scenes repeatedly return the Mistress and Butler roles here for forced relationship dialogue and plot setup.\n- The bedroom window can open directly into the Backstage Void rather than an exterior landscape, allowing the Troupe to insert or remove cast without using the mansion corridors. Its apparent outside view changes between acts according to the currently rendered set.\n- Major act transitions may be accompanied by Troupe personnel arriving to move the cast physically \"to the next scene,\" reinforcing that the mansion is one stage location rather than a complete residence.\n\n##### Garden\n- A formal walled garden containing roses, benches, a dry fountain, and a painted summer sky. It commonly serves as the first waking location for street-abducted participants or the initial scene for another principal role.\n- The garden presents as peaceful pastoral romance but is already part of the theatrical enclosure. Walls and gates do not connect to an ordinary exterior, and prolonged interaction here feeds directly into later romantic plot beats.\n\n##### Cathedral\n- A Gothic stone cathedral with stained-glass windows, pews, central aisle, and altar. It is the primary stage used for major confession scenes and the first explicit transition from romantic drama into lethal horror.\n- Carl's scripted confession culminates here: once the declaration of love is completed, the suicide compulsion takes control, he cuts his own throat, and his corpse is recycled into a Puppet that remains available for later scenes.\n- Windows above or near the altar can open directly into the Backstage Void. They are not ordinary windows but deliberate seams between rendered scene-space and the production layer behind it.\n\n**Carl**\n- A young male cast member assigned the principal romantic lead. He appears as a refined aristocratic man in courtly clothing and follows a structured progression toward declaring love to the Mistress.\n- Completing his confession triggers the production's central suicide rule. He cuts his own throat, dies on stage, and returns as one of the scenario's most dangerous Puppet combatants.\n- After conversion he may become visually absent outside specialized perception and attacks through rapid palm and knife-hand strikes. His movement produces identifiable rattling or joint sounds in the Backstage Void, while the Lake's water slows him to the same wading pace as other Puppets.\n- Destroying Carl does not resolve the plot. The production can continue generating or recycling further Puppets while its controlling apparatus remains active.\n\n##### Lake Stage\n- The final major set is a broad black lake beneath an artificial moon. The water is unnaturally warm and remains approximately waist-deep on the Mistress regardless of apparent distance from shore, making movement slow but preventing ordinary deep-water traversal.\n- Puppets are affected by the same water resistance and lose much of their open-ground speed while wading, making the lake the most manageable terrain for sustained engagement with a large pursuing group.\n- The Stage Light hangs directly above as the apparent moon, while its paired Switch is submerged near the mechanical center of the lake. The surrounding darkness is absolute beyond the illuminated water, allowing invisible or concealed Troupe combatants to approach from outside visual range.\n\n##### Backstage Void\n- A pitch-black navigational layer connecting sets. The surface feels like walking across a black canvas while travelers themselves remain faintly visible, but there are no landmarks, walls, light sources, or stable sense of vertical orientation.\n- Prior sets disappear completely when no longer observed, so looking back does not provide a reliable route. The Troupe uses the Void to reposition cast and combatants without crossing visible stage space.\n- Psychic pressure intensifies during prolonged traversal and can progress into migraine, disorientation, and bleeding from the eyes. Sound becomes disproportionately important because hidden attackers and Puppets may remain impossible to locate visually.\n\n**Backstage Troupe Actor**\n- At least one active Troupe combatant operates outside the rendered scenes and uses the Backstage Void's total darkness as concealment. The actor does not need to enter a visible role before attacking.\n- Its primary advantage is positional rather than structural invulnerability: ordinary cast cannot see it clearly in the Void, while it can use the production layer to approach between scenes and strike during transitions.",
-    "entities": "##### The Stage Light / Master Lamp\n- The Stage Manager's sun/moon lamp sustains roles, scenery, script, and transformations. Cast cannot normally reach it on foot or destroy it by firing ordinary weapons upward from a set.\n- The lamp has two direct failure points: breaking the bulb itself or switching off its paired control. Either disables the active lighting effect, halts further role transformation and scene regeneration, and removes much of the Stage Manager's authority over the current production.\n- The lamp can also be hijacked rather than destroyed. A consciousness-link or synchronization ability capable of joining a willing person's mind directly to the lamp can seize part of its scene-control function, transferring control of the production's principal instrument away from the Stage Manager.\n\n**The Switch**\n- The Switch is a plain mechanical control paired to the Stage Light; its physical form varies with the scene.\n- Turning it off plunges the production into total darkness and stops the Stage Light's active transformations. Destroying the Stage Light simultaneously destroys the Switch, and vice versa.\n- Darkness removes the lamp's structural control but does not erase Puppets already present, making immediate orientation and coordination necessary after activation.\n\n##### Stage Manager (극단주)\n- The unseen director responsible for Act Two's active staging and subordinate to the broader Theater Troupe leadership. It is never required to appear bodily within the production; its presence is expressed through announcements, the Master Lamp, scene editing, and interference with cast perception.\n- Ally Edit inserts the Stage Manager perceptually as a naturally trusted companion while transferring suspicion, psychic pressure, incoming psychological damage, or other hostile effects onto another member. An inexplicably trusted extra member plus causeless deterioration is the signature; check headcounts, assigned roles, and unexplained decline.\n\n##### Maid\n- A female human cast member locked into the household Maid role. She retains basic human biology and can briefly communicate normally when the active scene permits it, but role pressure repeatedly pulls her behavior back into the production.\n- Her successful outcome is survival through the end of the performance rather than fulfillment of a special combat objective. Killing her as though she were an anomalous resident only removes a recoverable cast member.\n\n##### Recast Cast\n- Abducted humans whose roles overwrite prior identity with genuine warmth, history, and emotional certainty. Strangers sincerely know entrants as daughter, parent, sibling, employer, or another relation while forgetting their old selves. Keep them alive until the production ends; forced immediate recognition is not the recovery method.\n- These cast members function as hostages embedded into the script. Treating them as monsters or disposable extras strengthens the production's tragedy and permanently sacrifices people who could otherwise leave when the act resolves.\n\n##### Puppets / Puppet Swarm\n- The enforcement population of the act, created from dead cast members as well as additional bodies generated between scene turns. Converted dead retain recognizable appearance and costuming but lose meaningful independent reasoning.\n- Puppets move with jerky, string-driven articulation, bent necks, and occasional stuttering vocal fragments while possessing enough physical strength to break walls or kill ordinary humans with a single heavy strike. On open ground they can close distance with extreme speed.\n- Flooded terrain is their principal environmental limiter. Waist-deep water forces them into the same awkward wading motion as the living cast and prevents the swarm from exploiting its full pursuit speed.\n- Their numbers remain effectively renewable while the Stage Light continues operating, making systematic extermination an attrition trap rather than a valid ending condition.",
