     "identity": "- The original orphanage possessed and biologically repurposed from within as one living multi-room organism, not replaced or relocated.\n- An introduced Pinwheel containment object fulfilled collected \"wishes to live\" from trapped staff and residents by fusing them into the building.",
     "entry": "- Under normal conditions, the orphanage operates as an ordinary Bureau children's facility and is freely accessible to staff and authorized visitors during standard hours.\n- Pinwheel activation locks doors, cuts communications, and collapses the interior inward. New arrivals may still enter the front door, but cannot reliably walk back out.\n- The Pinwheel was physically delivered into the building by hand and remains present inside. The activated state persists while the Pinwheel remains active on its display in the Teachers' Office.",
-    "space": "##### Characteristics\n- The transformed interior consists of narrow, dim corridors illuminated at irregular intervals by wall-mounted antique oil or brass lamps. Wooden framing remains visible, cold air streams from every opened room, and the walls themselves radiate a faint organic warmth.\n- The original room layout remains superficially recognizable, with shortened, tightened, repatterned corridors that nevertheless exceed exterior dimensions and expected distances.\n- Absorbed residents retain residual awareness and occasional distress in stat blocks or brief manifestations, but no coherent, recoverable humanity. Unabsorbed staff and children keep normal awareness, fear, and speech.\n\n##### Locations\n###### Main Entry Corridor\n- A dim, narrow, wooden-framed corridor immediately beyond the front door, with cold air flowing from open rooms and warm living walls. The first corpse-exhibit is usually encountered here: a forcibly converted staff member caught mid-transition, half embedded in the wall and half transformed into a brass or oil lamp. Wall-mounted lamps are themselves converted humans and can become ambulatory from the wall when provoked. The corridor is safe to traverse as long as surfaces are not deliberately touched, while faint residual breathing from absorbed humans can be heard from within the walls at the edge of perception.\n\n###### Numbered Living Rooms\n- Standard orphanage residential rooms numbered 101, 102, and onward. They are the least hostile interior structures because the absorption process has not fully converted them; their walls generally do not attack occupants who avoid deliberately touching them. First aid, survivor handoff, and temporary regrouping can be conducted here.\n\n**Surviving Children**\n- Ordinary elementary-age children with no anomalous talent or combat capacity and extreme baseline panic. They tend to cluster inside barricaded living rooms under the protection of a surviving teenager or teacher.\n\n###### Teachers' Office\n- A child-written \"Teachers' Room\" sign marks the small office. Its doorway blade can cut the first entrant's throat; account for the trap before passage. A desk against the wall holds the active Pinwheel and adjacent recorder. No other hostile entity is present while it is active; this office is the building's reactor room.\n\n**Pinwheel — Containment Core**\n- An ordinary toy pinwheel on bamboo, spinning without wind in the Teachers' Office. A tool without self-awareness or agency, applying the audible-wish inversion above with maximally destructive interpretation.\n- Retrieving and stopping the Pinwheel, extracting it from the building, or destroying it deactivates the house-scale anomaly. The orphanage returns to its normal geometry, route seals release, and living flesh-walls relax back into inert plaster. Personnel already converted into furniture remain converted.\n\n**Wish Recorder**\n- A small audio recorder positioned beside the Pinwheel. It continuously loops a high-pitched male voice: \"State your wish. If you ask to be saved, I will save you. That wound on your neck—if you wish, I will save you. If this continues, you will die. You only have one life, don't you?\"\n- Its purpose is to provoke trapped civilians and staff into verbalizing wishes and feeding them into the Pinwheel. The recorder is not necessary for the anomaly to function but dramatically accelerates the capture rate.\n\n###### Safe Room\n- The orphanage's mandated emergency shelter, where teachers instructed children to gather during the incident. The One Made of Many waits inside instead. The door operates on an internal timer and automatically opens shortly after a party approaches, releasing the entity into the corridor.\n\n**One Made of Many**\n- A composite flesh-wall entity formed from every in-house person who verbally wished for rescue. Its body is approximately corridor-scale in both height and width and must physically squeeze through doorways. The exterior is slick with blood and studded with protruding human arms, legs, fingers, eyes, and faces that continue moving and groaning.\n- It slides steadily through the corridors rather than charging. A closed narrow door prevents the full mass from passing but does not prevent portions of its body from forcing through openings to grab targets. Its speed is only a steady crawl, but it cannot be outrun indefinitely inside a finite corridor system.\n- Stopping the Pinwheel causes the entire composite to collapse back into its constituent humans immediately. Some components may survive the reversal, although most do not. Overlapping voices from inside repeatedly cry, \"Please save us\" and \"Please let all of us get out\".\n\n###### Director's Office\n- Contains miscellaneous small oddities personally collected by the director, presumed to be Bureau-adjacent objects of interest. No specific active anomaly among them is established.\n\n###### Attic\n- A clandestine hangout used by the children as a social space. It contains no active anomaly and can serve as a temporary triage point when the main corridor is actively hostile.",
