    "fields": {
     "identity": "##### Otherworld and Capture\n- A self-contained pocket-otherworld, not an overlay on a real site: an operating human-meat factory within a wider monster civilization. Industrial processing turns captured humans into cuts and labeled cans shipped by truck. Leaving the building reaches that civilization, not reality.\n- Entry begins at an anomalous real-world butcher shop. Ordinary-looking display meat resolves into unmistakable hands, calves, feet, eyes, ears, or similar human cuts. Capture has already begun: distorted vision gives way to waking on the toilet in the factory's eighth, innermost stall. No conscious boundary-crossing occurs; drawing a weapon or fleeing after recognition does not reliably interrupt transfer.\n- A saturated red sky is completely covered by enormous writhing intestines, like the inside of a gigantic viscera sphere. No visible sun, moon, or natural day/night cycle permits ordinary timekeeping. Worn, rusted buildings, roads, trucks, docks, fixtures, and machinery otherwise approximate human industrial equivalents.\n- Cleavers strike chopping blocks over conveyor and rail noise; rotting meat, old blood, yellow rendered fat, and rust fill the air, and grease and blood slick the floors. Survival depends on physical stealth, work-cycle timing, cover, and scent rather than contradictory rules or cognitive traps. No formal rulebook exists.",
     "entities": "##### Workforce and Work Cycle\n- Butchers are bloated bipeds with distended bellies, elephant-pillar legs, deeply wrinkled canary-yellow hide, and no eyes, nose, or ears. Their sole facial feature is a lipless mouth of discolored teeth dripping pus-like saliva. Rotting rag wraps and large rusted Chinese-style cleavers complete their appearance.\n- These are workers, not dedicated wardens. Running machinery keeps most at processing stations and connecting corridors relatively clear. Loud rattling means shutdown and break time: they roam corridors, restrooms, docks, and grounds, use toilets, rinse cleavers in bowls, and perform ordinary work tasks. They neither normally garrison the arrival stall nor interfere with the notebook.\n- Smell is their primary tracking sense. Repeated sniffing and barking breaths signal a human scent gradient; prolonged movement increases exposure. Thorough coating in Disposal Room waste is the strongest documented countermeasure and suppresses tracking for an extended period.\n- Ordinary humans are badly outmatched: point-blank handgun rounds flatten against hide, a grapple-and-slam can break an adult spine, and one cleaver stroke severs an arm. A blow inside the softer open mouth can cause a flinch, not a demonstrated kill. They outrun ordinary humans on open ground and cooperate in pairs or groups to restrain and process victims.\n- Exceptionally powerful anomalous force can kill many Butchers and collapse plant sections. Sustained overt combat can draw stronger monsters from the surrounding world, and destruction alone cannot defeat the regenerating Core.",
-    "space": "##### Arrival Base and Survivor Notebook\n- The insertion restroom has eight tiled stalls. An extractor fan may be the only sound before the main corridor is entered. It is a temporary base, never fully safe: Butchers periodically enter. Leave the stall door slightly ajar, stand silently on the toilet, and feign vacancy; fully closing it invites scrutiny. An apparently empty stall can still be entered by chance.\n- Notebook and pen sit on the eighth stall's toilet tank. The book persists across occupants and deaths but vanishes back to the stall if carried outside the bathroom. The space also repairs sabotage, including the destroyed partitions described in entry 16. Butchers seem neither to see nor police the book or spawn point; no arrival ambush is staged.\n- This is the only persistent human guide. Verified observation, inference, fear, and untested escape theories coexist; it is reference, not gospel. Preserve the different voices and their evidentiary limits.\n\n1. Park Mincheol, born 1999: monsters outside, \"kidnappers but definitely not people\". A will: farewell to his parents and a contact number for the finder.\n2. Kim Seoyun, born 2002: silently crying in a stall, too afraid to move; contact number and farewell to her parents.\n3. A young child: \"Mincheol-hyung, Seoyun-nuna, where are you, I'm scared, save me, I miss my mom.\" Successive victims read one another but never meet.\n4. Kim Seunghyun, born 1989: browsed a butcher shop, woke here, deduces teleportation. Once wanted to die, now only wants to live. Discovers the book cannot leave, resolves to scout and report, leaves a contact number.\n5. Sharp anonymous writer: deletes all earlier contact numbers (\"what if those things read this and do something with it\"); allows only name and birth year as identifying details. Observes one victim at a time and asks whether death or departure brings the replacement. Promises findings and \"see you on the World Politics Board if I get out\". Returns after nearly being caught: bathroom seems a base, check outside before leaving; corridor leads to a filthy, horrific meat plant. Will find the building exit.\n6. A parent, \"My son, Mom will stay strong\": monsters process meat during work and roam on breaks, much like humans. Resolves to determine their schedule.\n7. A third-grader, interrupted mid-word: \"I ran away and got into the bathr...\"\n8. Tactical writer: disproves total bathroom safety after surviving in the adjacent stall by pretending absence. Do NOT fully close the door; an occupied-looking stall may be checked or called into, and a human cannot answer monster speech. Leave it ajar, stand on the toilet. Entry into an \"empty\" stall remains luck.\n9. Despair: hanging meat is human; capture makes everyone meat. Intends to end it in the grinder. This records despair, not an escape finding.\n10. Despair: vast quantities of rail-borne meat are all human, none alive. Why does government not know of so many missing? This cannot be reality. Proposes the grinder as an exit, \"like dying in a nightmare wakes you, but being caught by the monster means no escape\", and intends to try. NEVER verified by a surviving record.\n11. Tactical audio cue: loud rattling means machines STOPPING, breaks and roaming beginning; return to the bathroom fast. Running machinery means work. No clocks or windows for measuring time.\n12. \"Is this even real? The notebook not leaving is wild.\"\n13. \"I can't do this.\"\n14. Critical finding: \"This is not our world. There IS an exit door, but it only leads out of this BUILDING. This whole place is not our world. It is the world these monsters live in. Getting out of the factory is meaningless.\" Ends: \"I'm hungry.\"\n15. Despairs at 14, will verify the exit, then find the grinder if it fails. Draws a partial facility map including the grinder; a later victim verifies the map.\n16. Saboteur: boasts of smashing every partition to expose and kill future arrivals, mocking the readers. The bathroom subsequently repairs itself.\n17. Rallying writer: finds the bathroom intact, condemns 16. If the exit is not home, another return must exist: \"we arrived here suddenly, so there must be a way back, that is how the world works\". Vows to struggle and post on the World Politics Board after escape. \"Let's keep going.\"\n18. Blank: the next victim's turn.\n\n##### Factory Route\n###### Main Corridor and Processing Line\n- Beyond the restroom, a wide, dark, filthy corridor has open and closed processing doors. Freight stacks, crates, machinery housings, and clutter provide cover; open doorways expose the traveler.\n- Inbound Limbs-Amputation / Hanging Room: Butchers restrain living captives, remove all four limbs, and apply a bucket substance to stumps to prevent immediate fatal blood loss. Hooks through both trapezius muscles suspend them on the rail, which normally holds dozens to about one hundred bodies.\n- Saw Tunnel: a short black conveyor passage with rotating circular saws on every side. Hook orientation determines entry; bulk meat emerges for further processing.\n- Disassembly Room: roughly twenty Butchers at normal staffing chop human carcasses, bone included, with their cleavers along powered rails. Blood, tissue, bone fragments, and yellow fat cover stations and floor.\n- Canning / Packing Room: meat is shoveled into small cans, sealed, marked with unreadable monster-language labels and stylized meat pictures, boxed in thirty-count lots, and handcarted to the outbound dock.\n\n###### Disposal Room and Grinder\n- Mountains of rotting tissue and bone contain victims' clothing, wallets, identification, photographs, and other belongings. A refuse handcart stands nearby. Roll thoroughly in this waste for the scent-masking protection described above; mere movement through the factory makes human odor increasingly dangerous.\n- The nearby Grinder Room, relatively close to insertion, holds one enormous industrial grinder. In the original layout, a side lever starts it, and full destructive operation takes time. The notebook's nightmare-awakening theory is unverified; do not turn its despair into a confirmed return procedure.\n\n###### Docks, Yard, and Kim Sung-jin\n- Front Gate / Outbound Delivery Dock: trucks reverse in; workers handcart canned-meat boxes into their beds. Hiding aboard is physically possible but carries the victim farther into the monster world, not automatically home.\n- Rear Gate / Inbound Supply Dock: trucks deliver humans. A lone driver commonly leaves the cab to open and unload the bed, creating the documented opportunity to steal an inbound vehicle.\n- The flat yard mixes dirt with flesh-like villi. Debris and refuse offer limited cover; the open plain beyond favors fast Butcher pursuers. A rear outlet continuously discharges greywater with no visible contamination, drinkable when no safer source is available.\n- Kim Sung-jin is a long-term civilian survivor, not an entity. He hides in a hand-dug spider hole with a salvaged monster light, eating outside villi and drinking wastewater. Isolation has caused screaming fits, finger-chewing, and abrupt mood reversals, yet he knows Butcher behavior, machine and bell timing, truck schedules, outbound product traffic, and the rear supply lane.",
