     "identity": "##### Arrival and Operating Cycle\n- A full-size discount hypermarket modeled on a defunct chain, rebuilt by something that understands retail behavior better than humans. LOOKYMART spelling, distorted logos, and imperfect old uniforms are subtly wrong. The mid-2000s Korean interior is excessively faithful: linoleum, dated promotions, fluorescent lights, repetitive pop, checkout lanes, liquor, household shelves, food counters, escalators, restrooms, and opening/closing announcements.\n- It manifests generally within several kilometers of demolished former branches. Dusk/evening pedestrians casually seeking a mart or convenience shop, without navigating to a known business, may find an aged neighborhood store whose local sign flickers into LOOKYMART.\n- Entry during business hours traps them. Some remember the doorway; others first recall standing mid-aisle with a cart. Nominal hours are about 10:00–23:00, but an early 20:30 closing may be announced. The first closing after arrival is the critical deadline.\n- During business, crowds shop, employees work, registers function, and recognized customers can theoretically reach normal exits. After closing, doors and shutters change, escalators stop or become hazards, darkness spreads, and employees distort. Entire wings may be dark with intermittent fluorescent bursts.\n- Hundreds have disappeared over decades. Survivors exist, but frequently exploited escape routes adapt and decay. Receipt-disguised survivor notices, which staff usually overlook, may be obsolete. Extract stable principles rather than trust every maneuver. No permanent destruction, sealing, or prevention of new entry is known.",
     "survival": "##### Status, Debt, and Exit Requirements\n- Best protection is a legitimate purchase with Lookymart vouchers, crudely issued in ₩10,000 and ₩1,000 denominations. Korean won is invalid. Registers may give voucher change and recognize the purchaser as a customer.\n- A secondary disguise is one item already paid for by another shopper, quietly taken and carried. This can fool an exit guardian but is unsafe if theft provokes its owner, a crowd, or staff; it grants less protection than one's own completed payment.\n- Customer recognition/disguise AND blocking the guardian's sight are separate requirements. Use an opaque object during doorway passage. Neither valid status with unobstructed sight nor a blinded guardian facing an unauthorized entrant suffices.\n- A properly paid customer leaving by the lawful route has stronger institutional protection: shoppers or trapped civilians openly attacking or obstructing them risk staff intervention.\n- Invalid payment, unpaid stock-taking or consumption, food-court breaches, and certain display hazards can create debt and inventory classification. Debt becomes an enduring status for long-stay victims rather than a realistically payable sum.\n- Inventory processing is routine departmental work: live grinding demonstrations, butchering/grilling as event stock, compression into living paper-thin advertisements, or folding into staff stock for later employee deployment.\n- Walk and behave like a shopper, avoid real names, shouting, harassment, open fights, damage, and public scenes. Calm retail etiquette reduces enforcement interest; anomalous merchandise is not free.",
-    "entities": "##### Shoppers and Contact\n- Past-Era Shoppers repeat historical shopping behavior in dated clothes, old mobile phones, and obsolete trend conversations as though the original chain still exists. They are nonviolent and usually cannot perceive entrants properly, attributing nearby movement to accidents.\n- Physical contact can expose the entrant and carried objects to them, producing panic at what previously moved invisibly. Deliberate low-traffic contact may enable communication or recruitment; accidental crowd contact may summon screaming, staff, and onlookers. They are deeply integrated and not reliably extractable.\n- Long-Term Trapped Civilians retain awareness but suffer malnutrition, mutilation, stock contamination, and unresolved debt. They recognize vouchers, food, equipment, and escape attempts and may plead, mob, rob, attack, or call staff against rivals. Avoid displaying an exit opportunity among them; proper paid-customer departure invokes the institutional limit on their interference.\n\n##### Lookymart Staff\n- Daytime workers wear shirts, blouses, aprons, caps, and name tags while cashiering, stocking, assisting, patrolling, and repeating formulaic service phrases.\n- After hours their hollow, balloon-like bodies stretch uniforms; limbs bend unnaturally, heads overrotate, and waddling jerks make rubbery squeaks and creaks.\n- They enforce payment and retail order, responding to disturbance, unpaid eating, harassment, damaged property, and interrupted service. Normal-customer behavior is safer than visible panic or intrusion.\n- Stable departure priorities remain: find and critically read a receipt notice, track closing time, obtain accepted customer status without debt or commotion, preserve access to 1F, and obstruct the guardian's sight while crossing before the first closing when possible.",
+    "entities": "##### Shoppers and Contact\n- Past-Era Shoppers repeat historical shopping behavior in dated clothes, old mobile phones, and obsolete trend conversations as though the original chain still exists. They are nonviolent and usually cannot perceive entrants properly, attributing nearby movement to accidents.\n- Physical contact can expose the entrant and carried objects to them, producing panic at what previously moved invisibly. Deliberate low-traffic contact may enable communication or recruitment; accidental crowd contact may summon screaming, staff, and onlookers. They are deeply integrated and not reliably extractable.\n- Long-Term Trapped Civilians retain awareness but suffer malnutrition, mutilation, stock contamination, and unresolved debt. They recognize vouchers, food, equipment, and escape attempts and may plead, mob, rob, attack, or call staff against rivals. Avoid displaying an exit opportunity among them; proper paid-customer departure invokes the institutional limit on their interference.\n\n##### Lookymart Staff\n- Daytime workers wear shirts, blouses, aprons, caps, and name tags while cashiering, stocking, assisting, patrolling, and repeating formulaic service phrases.\n- After hours their hollow, balloon-like bodies stretch uniforms; limbs bend unnaturally, heads overrotate, and waddling jerks make rubbery squeaks and creaks.\n- They enforce payment and retail order, responding to disturbance, unpaid eating, harassment, damaged property, and interrupted service. Normal-customer behavior is safer than visible panic or intrusion.",
     "space": "##### Floors and Service Areas\nThe accessible building has one basement and three nominal above-ground floors; the event floor exceeds its exterior volume.\n\n###### B1 / 1F and Exit Guardians\n- B1 holds snack/liquor aisles, coolers, warehouse shelving, checkouts, and lower escalator links. Densely crowded 1F Daily-Essentials sells cosmetics, kitchenware, dishes, cleaners, disinfectant, and appliances.\n- Street-facing 1F glass doors are the canonical normal exit. Door Ghosts stay fixed there during business, controlling passage rather than patrolling or responding to distant disturbances. Their appearance is recognizably wrong and not usefully describable; avoid prolonged observation. Stationary does not mean harmless.\n- After closing, daytime restrictions lapse and control changes, including the Founder Standee: a living, flattened blond middle-aged founder of the original Luckymart chain, fused as a life-size cardboard display into the exit structure. Eyes track, tears flow, and the mouth tries muffled speech.\n- Direct eye contact with the Standee without cover brings capture. Opaque sight obstruction can permit passage only under otherwise valid conditions; closing changes the exit problem rather than removing it.\n\n###### 2F Food Court\n- General merchandise, restrooms, water facilities, and a large indoor food court share the floor. Stall signs can stay lit amid surrounding darkness. Kitchen sounds and food appear without visible cooks.\n- This is the most reliable rest area because staff normally do not patrol it after hours, conditional on not sitting over approximately one hour without ordering. Prefer stalls not prominently serving meat.\n- Follow each stall's recognized transaction rules. Eating ordinary mart stock outside permitted conditions, especially outside the Food Court without settlement, creates debt rather than free nourishment.\n\n###### 3F Event-Promotion Floor\n- The nominal top floor exceeds exterior dimensions: thousands of temporary venues across hundreds of square kilometers of non-Euclidean event maze. Preserve the route to the downward escalator; losing it can mean permanent entrapment.\n- Camping Event tents, folding chairs, sleeping bags, mock fires, and equipment offer comparatively quiet hiding. Small groups can remain concealed for extended periods if silent.\n- Flesh-for-Sale Event rows of commercial grills barter cut pieces of living human bodies for valid vouchers. Mechanically valid currency comes at extreme human cost; this is not routine resupply.\n- The otherwise ordinary Electronics Sales Corner may receive equipment carried across the false 4F threshold, without its owner.\n\n###### False 4F / Staff Doors / After-Hours Escalators\n- Officially there is no 4F. Intermittent third-floor emergency doors labeled \"4F\" lead into a non-mart threshold; every confirmed entrant disappeared, with only equipment returning. Do not use them as exits.\n- Staff-only iron doors across floors lead to stock/break rooms, processing, and storage of compressed workers or civilian inventory between cycles.\n- Restrooms are dangerous during active staff cleaning: entry counts as interference with service and can provoke immediate enforcement.\n- Halted escalators become ambush lanes, with staff under inspection coverings or sliding soundlessly along unmoving steps. Check before committing. Downward access matters more than upward exploration because viable exits are below the event floor.\n- Missing the first closing changes the objective to survival until a usable business period returns. Expect degraded lights, altered staff and guardians, and hazardous floor changes; old after-hours escape accounts are unreliable."
