ome. Sit.\"\n\n<img=Jyaldi_default>\n\n\"The World Politics Forum. Publicly, it is known as an asylum ward. Bureau investigation found the majority of its users to be the mentally ill.\"\n\nThe majority. Which meant there were those who did not belong to it.\n\n\"Ordinary people using it for fun. They make up about nine tenths.\"\n\nWhich left a filtered, distilled one tenth. Asked what they were, Jyaldi shrugged.\n\n\"Who knows.\"\n\n\"They are the ones even the Bureau cannot trace. Whether they are actual humans. Or anomalous phenomena. Or the Fake Bureau's internet division. Nobody knows.\"\n\nJyaldi nodded and continued.\n\n\"They could be some other suspicious group entirely. One thing is certain. Among the countless false scraps of anomalous information posted to the Board, some are real, and the real ones are posted by that distilled one tenth.\"\n\nTo the question of whether they were dangerous, the answer was short.\n\n\"It depends. But not at present, it seems.\"\n\nAnd so that day's work began.\n\n*     *     *\n\nNothing that could be called a major incident broke out afterward.\n\nIt was all large-scale perception-suppression work spawned by the vampire incident, day after day of typing in the office. Spare hours went to the training facility and marksmanship practice.\n\nLiving that routine, the commutes repeated.\n\nThe dusk of one such commute home. Perhaps from walking with nerves strung tight, hunger set in.\n\nAt times like this, the answer was meat. Working for the Bureau meant money was no worry. The plan was to buy some hanwoo at a nearby butcher shop and grill it at home, and so the search led to a butcher's that sold hanwoo.\n\nThe butcher shop's signature pink lighting warmed the heart.\n\nDrawn in as if charmed, the display case and its cuts came into view. For hanwoo, surely ribeye. Enough to eat one's fill.\n\nExcept.\n\nThe meat was more than a little wrong.\n\nA vacuum-packed human hand.\n\nIt sat displayed in the case.\n\nBeside it, a 