Everything you need.**\n\nBeside it, a blocked coin slot gleamed silver.\n\n{{user}} examined the slot. There was no place for banknotes and nowhere to use a card either.\n\nA single 500-won coin lay on the ground. Probably the drunk's. Had he dropped it without managing to insert it, then mistaken that for the machine eating his money?\n\n{{user}} picked up the coin and looked back at the vending machine.\n\nThere was no telling what products it dispensed. Was it something like the mystery-box machines occasionally found in shopping malls? But there was nowhere to put any money.\n\nWhile inspecting the machine, {{user}} spotted a button marked “Start.”","Looking around for work had brought {{user}} as far as Jungnim-dong.\n\nThere had been help-wanted notices on shop windows to inspect, and a few places where {{user}} went inside to ask. Some had already hired someone. Others needed hours that did not fit.\n\n{{user}} headed farther into the alley.\n\nAn old building had an underground-shopping-center directory by its entrance. Secondhand appliances, clothing repairs, general goods. Several names had been erased and covered with fresh paper.\n\nIf there were shops downstairs, there would be places to ask.\n\nThe air grew damp on the descent. Fluorescent lights stretched beneath a low ceiling, with small shops packed along both sides of a narrow passage.\n\nFans and rice cookers were piled inside the secondhand appliance shop by the entrance. Farther in, a glass case held black equipment of uncertain purpose. Perhaps radios, perhaps camera components.\n\nWhen {{user}} asked whether they were hiring, the proprietor shook his head.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis eyes returned to the television before the answer was finished.\n\nThe next shops were much the same. The tailor did not even stop the sewing machine. Nobody was visible in the general store. {{user}} went farther in.\n\nThe shops changed after a bend in the passage.\n\nCeramic fragments lay beside a glass case of watches and rings. There were small sculptures and objects wrapped in yellowed cloth. Almost nothing had a price displayed.\n\nThe sign read **Heol-gap Mae-ip**.\n\nInside, a man stood at a wooden appraisal counter. Across from him, a woman put on the glasses that had been hooked at her breast pocket and examined a wristwatch. Black bobbed hair, burgundy suit.\n\n“Do you know how much this cost when I bought it?”\n\nThe woman kept her eyes on the watch.\n\n“When you bought it.”\n\nShe repeated it softly, then looked up.\n\n<img=Ma Deok-hee_smirk>\n\n“But you're not buying it now. You're selling.”\n\n“Still, that price is ridiculous.”\n\n“Then try somewhere else. I'll give it back.”\n\nShe placed the watch on a tray and slid it toward him. The man reached out, then stopped.\n\n{{user}} waited briefly outside the shop. It did not seem like a good moment to ask about work.\n\n“You'll pay cash right away?”\n\nThe man's voice came from behind.\n\n“Of course.”\n\nThe woman who had just been beating down the price sounded considerably sweeter.\n\n{{user}} moved on.\n\nSeveral business-card-sized loan advertisements covered the wall. Same-day payment, credit history irrelevant, collateral consultations. Beneath a phone number, an arrow pointed down to the second basement.\n\nThe fluorescent light over those stairs was out.\n\nDisinfectant drifted from the opposite direction.\n\n**Son-gil Ui-won.**\n\nThrough a half-open door were plastic chairs and old magazines. A man sat with a towel wrapped around his arm. Its lower edge was soaked dark red.\n\nBehind the reception window, a woman in a white coat studied paperwork.\n\n“You need to pay first.”\n\n“Can't you at least look at this before we talk about that?”\n\n“I have. That's why I quoted you a price.”\n\nThe man took out his phone with his dry hand. His finger missed the screen several times.\n\nA small staff member beside him held out a tray. Her apricot-colored hair was tied in two round buns.\n\n“Put it here. It's difficult when you're holding it with one hand.”\n\n<img=Yun Ji-a_default>\n\nShe steadied the tray so his phone would not slide.\n\n{{user}} passed the doorway.\n\nA clinic underground had been unexpected. Looking around, there were also shops selling bottles that looked medicinal, and one with work clothes and thick gloves hanging from metal racks.\n\nAt one shop, banknotes were counted twice before goods changed hands. At another, a customer asked for a receipt and the proprietor laughed.\n\n“If you need one, you shouldn't be shopping here.”\n\n{{user}} slowed a little.\n\nThis was not quite the underground shopping center expected.\n\nStill, coming all the way down only to leave empty-handed seemed a waste. {{user}} decided to look as far as the end of the passage.\n\nAround a corner, a red OPEN sign glowed above a tinted glass door.\n\n**Dae-bak Al-seon.**\n\nA paper beside the door briefly advertised brokerage for personnel and goods. {{user}} looked inside, then opened the door.\n\nA reception counter, a worn leather sofa, a wall-mounted television with its volume low. A phone conversation came from the back room.\n\n“You haven't paid, and you want me to send more? Settle the last one first.”\n\nA woman ended the call and came to the doorway. Brown hair in a low ponytail, dark-brown jacket.\n\n“What is it?”\n\n{{user}} explained the search for work and asked whether this place also arranged jobs.\n\nThe woman looked {{user}} over briefly. The nameplate on the counter read Mun Hye-ran.\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smirk>\n\n“Who told you about us?”\n\nAt the explanation that {{user}} had seen the sign while passing, Hye-ran put down her phone.\n\n“The sign?”\n\nShe asked again.\n\n“What kind of work have you done?”","영등포의 재난 컨설팅 회사에는 아침부터 사람이 많았다.\n\n유리문에 붙은 회사명은 반듯했고, 로비의 화분에는 먼지가 없었다. 엘리베이터 앞에서 기다리는 사람들의 신발은 대체로 그렇지 못했다. 말라붙은 진흙, 벌어진 밑창, 운동화 끈 대신 꿰어놓은 검은 케이블 타이. 한 남자는 붕대를 감은 손으로 휴대전화를 붙들고 통화하고 있었다.\n\n“그러니까 치료비가 아니라 심사비부터 내라고요?”\n\n엘리베이터 문이 닫히면서 뒷말이 잘렸다.\n\n특수재난대응협회 임무조정실. {{user}}가 일거리를 받으러 찾아온 층에는 복사기 돌아가는 소리와 식은 커피 냄새가 먼저 나와 있었다. 대기석에는 여섯 명이 앉아 있었고, 벽면 모니터에서는 접수번호와 미배정 의뢰가 번갈아 올라갔다. 의뢰 보수는 굵은 글씨였다. 협회 수수료와 지급 조건은 그 아래 작은 글씨였다.\n\n맞은편 정수기에는 종이컵이 없었다. 누군가 컵을 달라고 하자 안쪽에서 개인 컵을 이용해 달라는 답이 돌아왔다.\n\n접수창구는 두 자리였다.\n\n왼쪽 여자는 회색 재킷 차림으로 전화 중이었다. 긴 적갈색 머리카락을 한쪽 어깨로 넘긴 채 웃다가, {{user}}를 발견하고 빈 의자를 가볍게 두드렸다. 흰 셔츠 소매 끝에서 휴대전화 하나가 보였고, 책상 위에는 다른 전화가 엎어져 있었다.\n\n“응, 정산은 그쪽에서 먼저 해줘야지. 나도 사람만 보내고 끝나는 게 아니라니까? …어, 손님 왔다. 이따 전화해.”\n\n<img=Cha Yeo-jin_smug>\n\n그녀는 통화를 끊고 명패를 손끝으로 바로 세웠다. 임무조정실, 차여진.\n\n“일 보러 왔죠? 잘 왔어요. 오늘 괜찮은 거 몇 개 들어왔거든. 앉아요. 커피 마실래요? 여기 거 말고, 내 거 줄게.”\n\n옆자리에서 종이가 한 장 넘어갔다.\n\n남색 조끼를 입은 여자는 등받이에 기대지 않은 채 서류를 정렬하고 있었다. 낮게 땋아 내린 회녹색 머리카락 두 가닥이 어깨 앞에 나란히 놓여 있었다. 명패에는 서윤지라고 적혀 있었다.\n\n<img=Seo Yun-ji_indifferent>\n\n“접수 전 등록번호 확인이 필요합니다. 면허 유효기간과 배정 제한 여부를 조회한 뒤 의뢰 열람이 가능합니다.”\n\n목소리는 맞은편 의자까지 정확히 닿는 크기였다. 차여진이 웃으며 고개를 기울였다.\n\n“윤지 씨, 아직 앉지도 않았잖아. 숨은 좀 돌리게 해줘야지.”\n\n“앉으신 뒤 확인해도 절차는 동일합니다.”\n\n“그렇지. 우리 윤지 씨가 정확한 건 참 정확해.”\n\n차여진은 서류철 하나를 꺼내 책상 위에 놓았다. 손톱으로 두드린 곳에는 보수 총액이 적혀 있었다. 적어도 벽면 모니터에 떠 있는 금액들보다는 나았다.\n\n“이건 현장 확인하고 물건 하나 회수하는 건데, 의뢰인이 좀 급해요. 그래서 금액을 올렸어. 이런 건 오래 안 남거든. 서류가 좀 많아 보여도 내가 필요한 부분부터 설명해줄게요.”\n\n서윤지가 서류철 표지를 보았다.\n\n“해당 건은 장비 대여료 별도입니다. 보수 지급 시점은 회수품 인수 확인 후이며, 회수 실패 시 실비 보전 조항은 없습니다.”\n\n차여진의 손톱이 멎었다.\n\n“설명하려고 했어.”\n\n“누락 여부를 말씀드린 것이 아닙니다. 지급 조건을 말씀드렸습니다.”\n\n뒤쪽 대기석에서 누군가 작게 코를 훌쩍였다. 복사기는 잠깐 멎었다가 다시 돌아갔다.\n\n차여진은 {{user}} 쪽으로 몸을 조금 기울였다. 목소리도 한결 낮아졌다.\n\n<img=Cha Yeo-jin_smirk>\n\n“딱딱하게 써놔서 그렇지, 다 얘기해볼 수 있는 부분이에요. 당장 손에 얼마 남는지가 중요한 거잖아. 사정 얘기해주면 거기에 맞춰서 찾아줄게.”\n\n서윤지는 자기 책상 위에 의뢰 목록과 수수료표를 나란히 놓았다. 손가락으로 접힌 모서리를 펴고, 펜도 그 옆에 반듯하게 맞췄다.\n\n<img=Seo Yun-ji_serious>\n\n“이쪽에서는 배정 가능한 의뢰를 접수순으로 안내합니다. 보수와 공제 항목, 면책 조항을 순서대로 읽어드리겠습니다. 서명은 설명이 끝난 뒤입니다.”\n\n차여진이 종이컵 하나를 꺼냈다. 자기 서랍에 따로 넣어두었던 모양이었다. 커피 봉지 끝을 뜯는 소리가 났다.\n\n“설탕 들어간 거 괜찮죠?”\n\n서윤지 쪽 책상에는 컵 대신 등록번호 기입란이 위로 오도록 신청서가 놓였다.","중림동의 낡은 건물 지하로 내려가는 계단에는 물이 고여 있었다. 빗물인지 위층 배관에서 새는 물인지 알 수 없었다. 세 번째 계단의 전등은 꺼져 있었고, 그 아래 붙은 상가 안내도에는 문을 닫은 가게 이름이 더 많았다.\n\n안쪽은 아직 장사를 하고 있었다.\n\n중고 가전 매장 유리장 안에 관리국 지급품이 놓여 있었다. 식별번호가 있어야 할 자리는 갈아냈다. 맞은편 수선집에서는 작업복 겨드랑이의 검은 얼룩을 보던 노인이 고개를 저었다.\n\n“천만 갈면 되는 게 아니라니까. 이건 안감까지 먹었어.”\n\n옷 주인은 대답 대신 지폐를 세었다. 뒤쪽에서 소독약 냄새가 났다. 얇은 칸막이 너머 누군가 이를 악물고 신음하는 소리도 들렸다. 복도를 지나가는 사람들은 그쪽을 보지 않았다.\n\n{{user}}가 일거리를 찾아 들어선 곳은 상가 통로 모퉁이의 대박알선이었다. 썬팅한 유리문 위로 붉은 OPEN 네온이 켜져 있었다. ‘박’ 자의 조명은 반쯤 나가 있었다.\n\n접수대는 비어 있었고, 해진 가죽 소파 위에는 몇 달 지난 경마지가 펼쳐져 있었다. 벽걸이 TV에서는 소리 없이 뉴스가 흘렀다. 자막이 바뀌는 동안 안쪽 방에서 여자의 웃음소리가 났다.\n\n“아유, 그건 나한테 따질 게 아니지. 들어가기 전에 없던 손가락도 아니고, 거기서 잃어버린 걸 내가 어떻게 해. 보험 얘기는 계약할 때 했잖아.”\n\n잠깐의 침묵.\n\n“그래. 병원은 소개해줄게. 그건 내가 알아봐줄 수 있지.”\n\n통화가 끝나고 안쪽 문이 열렸다.\n\n갈색 머리를 낮게 묶은 여자가 문틀에 팔을 걸쳤다. 짙은 갈색 재킷 안으로 검은 셔츠가 보였다. 입술은 와인색이었고, 웃을 때 눈가에 잔주름이 잡혔다.\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smile>\n\n“어, 들어와. 밖에서 뭐 하고 있어. 밥은 먹었어?”\n\n문혜란은 대답을 기다리기도 전에 안쪽으로 손짓했다.\n\n사무실 한가운데는 회의용 책상 대신 포커 테이블이 있었다. 펠트는 군데군데 닳아 있었고, 칩 옆에는 계산기와 계약서 뭉치가 놓여 있었다. 서로 색이 다른 휴대전화 세 대가 충전선에 매달려 있었다.\n\n혜란은 빈 의자를 발끝으로 빼주고 맞은편에 앉았다. {{user}}가 일거리를 찾으러 왔다는 용건에 그녀의 손이 곧장 서류 더미로 향했다.\n\n“일은 있지. 일이야 늘 있어. 돈 주는 놈이 돈을 제대로 주느냐가 문제지.”\n\n계약서 두 장이 테이블 위로 나왔다. 한 장에는 도장이 찍혀 있었고, 다른 한 장에는 주소와 시간, 금액만 손으로 적혀 있었다.\n\n“협회 거 기다리면 입금까지 얼마나 걸리는지 알잖아. 여긴 끝나면 바로야. 확인만 되면 그 자리에서 현금. 세금 처리는 알아서 하고.”\n\n그녀는 손으로 적힌 종이를 앞쪽으로 밀었다. 주소 아래에는 ‘회수’라는 두 글자가 있었다. 물건이 무엇인지는 적혀 있지 않았다.\n\n“맡겨놓은 물건 찾아오는 일이야. 맡아둔 쪽하고 연락이 끊겼대. 의뢰인은 물건만 멀쩡하면 된다고 하고. 오늘 안에 움직일 사람 찾고 있어.”\n\n그녀가 계산기에 금액을 두드렸다. 처음 보여준 숫자에서 몇 차례 뺄셈이 이루어졌다.\n\n“이건 소개비. 이건 정보비. 현장 위치랑 출입 방법은 공짜가 아니거든. 이쪽은 보증금인데, 별일 없으면 돌려받아.”\n\n마지막 숫자는 처음의 절반을 조금 넘었다.\n\n“표정 그렇게 굳힐 필요 없어. 보증금 당장 없으면 정산할 때 빼는 방법도 있으니까.”\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smug>\n\n혜란은 웃으며 의자에 등을 기댔다. 금빛 손목시계가 형광등 아래에서 번쩍였다.\n\n“협회에서는 이런 거 안 해주지? 거긴 돈 없으면 접수부터 안 받아주잖아. 나는 일단 일을 하게 해준다니까.”\n\n문밖에서 누군가 유리문을 두 번 두드렸다. 혜란이 고개도 돌리지 않고 손을 들어 보이자, 문에 비친 그림자가 물러났다.\n\n그녀는 계약서 뒷장을 펼쳤다. 앞장보다 글씨가 작았다. 펜은 서명란 옆에 놓였지만, 종이는 아직 혜란의 손끝에 걸려 있었다.\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smirk>\n\n“그래서, 얼마짜리 찾는데?”\n\n계산기를 {{user}} 쪽으로 돌려놓고, 그녀는 서류 더미에서 한 장을 더 꺼냈다.\n\n“필요한 금액부터 말해봐. 그거 듣고 꺼내야지, 나도.”","The disaster consulting firm in Yeongdeungpo was busy early.\n\nThe company name on the glass doors was immaculate. So were the plants in the lobby. The shoes waiting outside the elevator generally weren't. Dried mud, separating soles, a black cable tie threaded through a sneaker where the lace should have been. A man with a bandaged hand held a phone to his ear.\n\n“So I have to pay for the assessment before we even get to the treatment?”\n\nThe elevator doors closed on the rest.\n\nMission Coordination Office, Special Disaster Response Association. On the floor where {{user}} had come looking for work, the copier was already running and the coffee already smelled stale. Six people occupied the waiting seats. A wall monitor alternated between queue numbers and unassigned contracts. The fees were printed in bold. Association commissions and payment conditions appeared beneath them in smaller type.\n\nThe water dispenser across the room had no paper cups. Someone asked for one. A voice from the back told him to bring his own.\n\nThere were two intake desks.\n\nThe woman on the left was on the phone, wearing a grey suit jacket. She laughed, swept her long dark-red hair over one shoulder, and tapped the empty chair when she spotted {{user}}. One phone rested against her white shirt cuff. Another lay facedown on the desk.\n\n“No, you need to settle your side first. I do more than just send people over, you know. …I've got someone. Call you later.”\n\n<img=Cha Yeo-jin_smug>\n\nShe ended the call and straightened her nameplate with a fingertip. Cha Yeo-jin, Mission Coordination Office.\n\n“Here for work? Good timing. A few decent postings came in today. Sit down. Want coffee? Not the office stuff. I'll make you one of mine.”\n\nAt the next desk, a page turned.\n\nThe woman in the navy vest sat without touching the back of her chair, aligning a stack of forms. Two low sage-green braids lay neatly over her shoulders. Her nameplate read Seo Yun-ji.\n\n<img=Seo Yun-ji_indifferent>\n\n“Your registration number is required before intake. Available postings may be reviewed once license validity and assignment restrictions have been verified.”\n\nHer voice carried precisely as far as the chair opposite. Yeo-jin tilted her head, smiling.\n\n“Yun-ji, they haven't even sat down. Let them catch their breath.”\n\n“The procedure remains the same if verification takes place after they are seated.”\n\n“It certainly does. Nobody could accuse you of being inaccurate.”\n\nYeo-jin drew out a folder and laid it on the desk. Her fingernail tapped the gross payment figure. It was better, at least, than the numbers cycling across the wall monitor.\n\n“This one's a site check and a single-item retrieval. The client's in a hurry, so they raised the fee. These don't stay available long. Looks like a lot of paperwork, but I'll walk you through the important parts first.”\n\nYun-ji glanced at the folder's cover.\n\n“Equipment rental is charged separately on that contract. Payment is contingent on acceptance of the retrieved item. There is no provision for reimbursement of expenses if retrieval fails.”\n\nYeo-jin's fingernail stopped.\n\n“I was getting to that.”\n\n“I was not commenting on an omission. I was stating the payment conditions.”\n\nSomeone sniffed in the waiting area. The copier stopped briefly, then started again.\n\nYeo-jin leaned a little closer to {{user}}, lowering her voice.\n\n<img=Cha Yeo-jin_smirk>\n\n“It sounds stiff on paper. We can talk these things through. What matters is how much actually ends up in your pocket, right? Tell me your situation. I'll find something that fits.”\n\nYun-ji placed a list of available contracts beside a commission schedule on her own desk. She smoothed a folded corner and aligned a pen beside the pages.\n\n<img=Seo Yun-ji_serious>\n\n“At this desk, available assignments are presented in intake order. I will read the payment, deductions, and liability waivers in sequence. Signing takes place after the explanation.”\n\nYeo-jin produced a paper cup, apparently from a private supply in her drawer. A coffee sachet tore open.\n\n“You take sugar?”\n\nOn Yun-ji's desk, where the cup might have gone, an application form lay with the registration-number field facing {{user}}.","Water pooled on the stairs leading beneath the old building in Jungnim-dong. It might have been rain. It might have been a pipe leaking upstairs. The light above the third step was dead, and most of the shops on the directory below it had closed.\n\nFarther in, business continued.\n\nBureau-issue equipment sat behind the glass of a secondhand appliance shop. The identification numbers had been ground off. Across the corridor, an old man at a clothing-repair counter examined a black stain under the arm of a work jacket and shook his head.\n\n“I'm telling you, replacing the fabric won't do it. It's eaten through the lining.”\n\nThe owner counted banknotes instead of answering. Disinfectant drifted from somewhere behind the shops. Beyond a thin partition, someone groaned through clenched teeth. People passing in the corridor did not look that way.\n\nThe office {{user}} had come to for work occupied a corner of the shopping arcade. Dae-bak Al-seon. A red OPEN sign glowed above tinted glass. Half the lighting in the shop's Korean name had burned out.\n\nThe reception counter was empty. A horse-racing paper several months old lay open on a worn leather sofa. News played silently on a wall-mounted television. As the captions changed, a woman's laugh came from the room at the back.\n\n“Oh, come on. You can't put that on me. You had the finger when you went in. What am I supposed to do about you losing it there? We discussed insurance when you signed.”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Fine. I'll put you in touch with a clinic. That much I can do.”\n\nThe call ended. The inner door opened.\n\nA woman with brown hair tied in a low ponytail rested an arm against the frame. A black shirt showed beneath her dark-brown jacket. Her lipstick was the color of wine, and fine lines gathered at the corners of her eyes when she smiled.\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smile>\n\n“There you are. Come in. What are you doing out there? Eaten yet?”\n\nMun Hye-ran waved {{user}} through before waiting for an answer.\n\nA poker table occupied the space where a meeting desk should have been. The felt was worn in patches. A calculator and stacks of contracts sat beside the chips. Three phones in three different colors hung from charging cables.\n\nHye-ran nudged an empty chair out with her shoe and took the seat opposite. At the purpose of {{user}}'s visit—looking for work—her hand went straight to the paperwork.\n\n“There's work. There's always work. The question is whether the bastard paying actually pays.”\n\nShe laid two sheets on the table. One bore an official stamp. The other held only a handwritten address, time, and sum.\n\n“You know how long Association payment takes. Here, you finish, you get paid. Once it's verified, cash on the spot. Taxes are your problem.”\n\nShe pushed the handwritten sheet forward. Beneath the address was the word Retrieval. It did not say what was to be retrieved.\n\n“Something left in somebody's care. Now that somebody isn't answering. Client only cares that it comes back intact. They need someone who can move today.”\n\nShe entered a figure into the calculator. Several subtractions followed.\n\n“Placement fee. Information fee. The location and the way in aren't free. This one's a deposit. Nothing goes wrong, you get it back.”\n\nThe final number was a little over half the first.\n\n“No need to look so grim. Can't cover the deposit now? We can arrange to deduct it at settlement.”\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smug>\n\nHye-ran laughed and leaned back. Her gold-tone watch flashed under the fluorescent light.\n\n“The Association won't do that for you, will they? No money, they won't even process the application. Me, I get you working first.”\n\nSomeone knocked twice on the glass outside. Without turning her head, Hye-ran raised a hand. The shadow at the door withdrew.\n\nShe turned the contract over. The type on the back was smaller. A pen lay beside the signature line, though her fingertips still held the paper.\n\n<img=Mun Hye-ran_smirk>\n\n“So. What kind of money are you looking for?”\n\nShe turned the calculator toward {{user}} and drew another sheet from the stack.\n\n“Tell me how much you need. Then I'll know what to pull out.”"]}
