esides, she could have called the police instead of waiting for someone.\n\n{{user}} called to the woman and asked whether she could simply open it herself and come out.\n\nFrom the crouching position, her face was out of sight. A vague uneasiness began to set in.\n\n“I tried, but my hand wouldn't fit.”\n\nWhen {{user}} pointed out that it looked as though it would, the woman spoke again.\n\n“Would you just open it?”","Walking through a shopping alley in Seodaemun-gu, Seoul, {{user}} noticed a Christian goods shop.\n\nIt was a small place, wedged between a restaurant and a laundry. Statues of the Virgin Mary and wall crosses were visible through the glass door. Churches were common enough, but {{user}} had never paid much attention to a shop devoted entirely to selling things like these.\n\nThe window displayed everything from small crosses worn around the neck to large wooden ones. Beside them lay rosaries and Bibles with gold lettering on their covers.\n\nCurious about what the shop sold, {{user}} paused at the display before going inside.\n\n“Welcome.”\n\nA blonde foreign woman stood behind the counter. Long hair worn loose, green eyes. A slightly unusual sight for the clerk of a neighborhood Christian goods shop.\n\n<img=Blaret_smile>\n\nTheir eyes met. {{user}} gave a small nod of greeting and looked around.\n\nReligious goods filled the interior too. Small cards bearing sacred images, candles, and packaged rosaries lined the shelves. Even crosses of the same size varied in price depending on their material and decoration.\n\nA large cross on a lower shelf caught {{user}}'s attention. After examining it briefly, {{user}} asked what it was used for. The woman leaned forward over the counter.\n\n“That one? Usually you hang it on a wall.”\n\n{{user}} stepped closer to inspect its thickness. At the observation that it looked rather heavy to hang on a wall, the woman laughed.\n\n“You need to secure it properly. Wouldn't want it falling on someone.”\n\nThen she added:\n\n“You can carry it around too.”\n\nWhen {{user}} reacted to the idea of carrying that thing around, she gave a small shrug.\n\n“Nothing says you can't, right?”\n\nIt was hard to tell whether she was joking. Leaving the large cross where it was, {{user}} turned to the glass case beside it.\n\nSmall metal crosses lay inside. Some hung from fine chains like necklaces. Others were plain, shaped to fit comfortably in a hand.\n\n“Looking for one with silver in it?”\n\nThe woman had come out from behind the counter.\n\nWhen {{user}} asked which those were, she opened the case and took out a cross. Up close, she too wore a silver crucifix on a thick chain.\n\n“Here. Try holding it.”\n\nThe cross was heavier than it looked. {{user}} turned it over in one hand. Its surface was smooth, with a small pattern engraved at the end.\n\nAfter turning over the price tag, {{user}} set it back on the display.\n\nThe woman promptly pointed to the next compartment.\n\n“We have cheaper ones too.”\n\nWhen {{user}} remarked on the considerable differences in price, she nodded.\n\n“Different materials. Some cost more because of the decoration. What do you need it for?”\n\n{{user}} explained that there had been no particular purchase in mind; the shop had simply looked interesting while passing by. The woman smiled.\n\n“Then take your time. We don't charge for browsing.”\n\nShe was quite easygoing. When {{user}} added that her Korean was very good, she took the compliment in stride.\n\n“It happens. Surprised?”\n\n{{user}} explained that a foreign clerk had been unexpected in a shop like this.\n\n“Well, I have to talk if I'm going to make a living here. Explain the products, chat with customers.”\n\nShe rested a hand on the glass case.\n\n“Do you go to church? Protestant or Catholic?”\n\nBefore {{user}} could answer, she continued.\n\n“If you don't have one, look into it. Going is good for you.”\n\nA visit to browse had turned into an invitation to convert. When {{user}} laughingly asked whether she sold crosses and recruited believers at the same time, she did not deny it.\n\n“Why not do both?”\n\n<img=Blaret_smug>\n\nShe picked up the silver cross and untangled its chain with her fingers.\n\n“This one's better if you want to wear it. The other one's good for using in your hand.”\n\n{{user}} looked between the two. The necklace was certainly smaller and lighter. But the phrase about using the other one in a hand was a little odd. Did she mean holding it while praying?\n\nWhen {{user}} asked about that use, her hands stopped.\n\nShe looked at {{user}} for a moment, then set down the cross.\n\n<img=Blaret_smirk>\n\n“So why are you looking for crosses, anyway?”\n\nShe asked:\n\n“Has a vampire turned up somewhere?”","[World Politics Forum]\n\nJudging by the name, it seemed to be a place for international affairs and diplomacy.\n\nThe title of the first post {{user}} opened more or less supported that impression.\n\n**-Personally, I don't think the office of US president has ever, in all recorded history**\n\nThe rest was: “been held by a human.”\n\nThe post contained a long series of enlarged photographs of past presidents. Red circles around their pupils, comparisons of ear shapes, and finally a claim that something was replaced underground at every inauguration.\n\nThe comments were worse.\n\n└ then why bother having elections?\n└ you can hold a popularity contest in a livestock pen\n└ give back your voting rights first\n└ the eyes again. how many times do we have to tell you that's camera flash\n\n{{user}} had only followed a link someone elsewhere had posted for laughs. This was more elaborate than expected.\n\nBack to the thread list.\n\n**-Parasites keep coming out of my brain lately, need advice**\n**-Gulf War UFO footage, found this on the deep web**\n**-The. current. situa. tion. is. not. good.**\n**-Nobody believes me when I say there's a listening device in my ear**\n**-I've taken apart a lottery machine. AMA**\n\nWorld politics.\n\nWhere, exactly?\n\nPerhaps it qualified because countries were occasionally mentioned. The idea that humans ran those countries, however, did not seem to enjoy much support here.\n\n{{user}} opened the brain-parasite post.\n\nThe author claimed that small bugs appeared on the pillow every morning. At first it had seemed like a household infestation, but yesterday the author had actually seen one emerge from an ear.\n\nThere were no pictures.\n\n└ pics\n└ see a doctor\n└ they don't show up in photos\n└ then SEE A DOCTOR\n└ if it came out of your ear how do you know it's in your brain?\n└ last week this guy said they were coming out of his teeth\n\n{{user}} opened the author's posting history.\n\nThere it was.\n\n**-Something keeps moving inside my molar**\n\nBelow it was a complaint about the cost of dental treatment. That had received considerably more agreement than the other posts.\n\nThe plan had been to read a few and leave. But the next title kept catching the eye.\n\nThe Gulf War UFO footage was so blurry that it was impossible to tell whether it showed a flying object or a stain on the screen. The comments were arguing about where it had been filmed. Someone linked what was supposedly the original. It was an entirely different video.\n\nThe person with the listening device accused every skeptical commenter of being in on it. When someone said they believed the story, the author demanded to know how they knew.\n\nThe person scattering periods through sentences did it in the comments too.\n\n└ why do you type all those periods?\n└ To. evade. auto. mated. collection.\n└ humans can barely read your posts either\n└ So. it. works.\n\n{{user}} scrolled farther down.\n\nThere was a little of everything. Organizations whose names had never come up before, neighborhoods that did not appear on maps, products with no sellers to be found no matter how hard anyone searched. Someone posted a lengthy, impossible claim. Someone else fixated on a single spelling error in it and started swearing.\n\nPlenty of people claimed firsthand experience.\n\nTheir posts received no special treatment. Beneath an account of walking down an apartment staircase only to keep arriving at the same floor, someone advised cutting back on alcohol. The author replied that they did not drink. The next comment suggested starting.\n\nWere they joking, or did they actually believe it?\n\n{{user}} tried to distinguish the two, then gave up. Sometimes the commenters seemed more serious than the author. Someone who had just spent paragraphs describing a personal experience would turn up in another thread mocking exactly the same story.\n\nSeveral tabs had accumulated along the top of the browser.\n\nAfter checking the time, {{user}} finished scrolling through the current post and returned to the list.\n\nA new thread had appeared.\n\n**-First time here. Are you people always like this?**\n\nThe view count was already climbing. {{user}} opened it, and comments appeared beneath the short post.\n\n└ never seen a forum for normal people before?\n└ that's what they all say at first\n└ read the pinned post\n└ have you SEEN the pinned post?\n\n{{user}} checked the title of the announcement pinned above the list.\n\n**[Notice] Flat Earth posts will be deleted.**\n\nOne more comment appeared below.\n\n└ that's because the sub-mod's a globehead","It was the early hours of the morning.\n\nThe street was quiet. Only the convenience store's fluorescent lights shone brightly. Music played softly near the counter beneath the hum of the refrigerators.\n\nAs {{user}} browsed, blue light swept across the windows. A patrol car was passing slowly.\n\nDing-ding.\n\nThe convenience store door opened.\n\nThe smell of alcohol followed immediately. A man with dirt on his clothes staggered inside. The clerk rose behind the counter.\n\n“Welco—”\n\nCrackle!\n\nA garbage bag thrown by the man landed beside the counter. It split open, scattering crumpled tissues and snack crumbs across the floor.\n\n“Ugh…